  "bodyText": "A 61-year-old male patient presenting with plaque psoriasis was initiated on Zolapamine at a first dose of 75 mg. Three days following the administration, the patient developed a larger and more painful area of induration, measuring approximately 8 cm, located at the abdominal injection site. This local reaction was accompanied by a low-grade fever reaching 38.1 degrees Celsius that persisted for one day. He was evaluated to rule out possible cellulitis, and an ultrasound confirmed soft-tissue edema without any abscess formation, while blood cultures returned negative. Due to the event, Zolapamine was held for one cycle and subsequently restarted at a reduced initial dose alongside premedication. Following this management strategy, the patient experienced no recurrence of the adverse reaction. The case provides insights into managing localized injection site reactions linked to this pharmaceutical product.",
